## 2. Two constructive strengthenings (assertions upgraded to constructions)
**(a) Pin-correcting operator exists — explicit basis.** The six pin functionals (f, f_X, f_Y at (±½,0)) are invertible on span{1, X, X², X³, Y, XY}: the (f, f_X)-block on {1, X, X², X³} is classical two-point cubic Hermite (invertible); the f_Y-block on {Y, XY} is the invertible 2×2 [[1, ½],[1, −½]]. The correction has degree ≤ 3, staying inside the pin-preserving degree-four class, and the operator norm claim in 4.1 becomes constructive.
**(b) Symmetry legitimizes the spectral toolkit.** R_r = ∇(P_r − P_r^{(4)}) is a gradient field, so DR_r is symmetric everywhere — Weyl and Davis–Kahan apply exactly as used, with no symmetrization loss. Worth stating because falsifier 4's audit would otherwise need to check the nonsymmetric variants.

